+---
+node: record-store
+---
+
+# Decision: Whole-Visit Revisions for Concurrent Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+The record store accepts visit submissions from tablets that can be offline for up to 48 hours. Workers independently correct the same visit after comparing field notes, and the server must not treat arrival order as evidence that one worker's observations are more authoritative.
+
+A visit is a complete scientific record: a plot reference, observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Some edits are scientifically related, such as revising a species identification and its cover estimate. Others are unrelated, such as correcting an observer name and adding a missing species. The server cannot reliably infer every relationship from field names alone.
+
+The concurrency policy determines the submission envelope, the shape of review work, and which visits can enter the weekly partner export. Tablet-sync and review-export are sized around that contract. Two policies are credible: whole-visit revisions and field-aware three-way merge. Both can retain every original submission, support offline capture, and produce the partner's existing columns. The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden, scientific safety, and explainability. Last-write-wins is excluded because it silently discards a competing edit, and locking the visit during editing is excluded because it blocks offline capture.
+
+The HLD's tenets bound the choice:
+
+- Do not silently discard a submitted edit.
+- Let field workers continue collecting observations without connectivity.
+- Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable.
+- Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+### Shared constraints
+
+These are required of either policy and do not discriminate between them:
+
+- Every accepted submission is retained; no edit is silently discarded.
+- Field workers can capture observations through the expected 48-hour disconnection.
+- The Friday CSV uses the partner's existing columns.
+
+No gate eliminates either option. The decision turns on the weighted criteria below.
+
+### Weighted criteria
+
+- **Scientific safety — major.** Tenets: *Do not silently discard a submitted edit*; *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*. The policy should avoid combining edits whose scientific relationship the system cannot establish. A policy that automatically combines related edits can produce a plausible but wrong current record.
+
+- **Review workload and export readiness — major.** Tenet: *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*. Every review item consumes coordinator time, and unresolved visits are held out of the weekly CSV. The policy's conflict granularity determines queue size and how many visits are ready by Friday.
+
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden — major.** Tenet: *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*. The policy needs to be buildable and maintainable by the team. Field-aware merge requires rules for each field shape, including observer lists and species entries. Whole-visit revisions require a revision graph and a resolution workflow. The species cover encoding and species-entry shape are not yet fixed, so merge rules would be built and maintained against a moving schema.
+
+- **Explainability and provenance — major.** Tenet: *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*. A coordinator, the science partner, or an auditor needs to be able to explain why an exported value is what it is. Whole-visit provenance is revision-level; field-aware provenance is field-level.
+
+- **Offline capture and retry simplicity — moderate.** Tenet: *Let field workers continue collecting observations without connectivity*. The tablet must retain enough state to submit after a long disconnection and to retry safely. Whole-visit revisions need a base revision ID and a complete draft; field-aware merge needs a base snapshot and structured changes.
+
+- **Submission size and network cost — moderate.** Full snapshots are larger than change sets. At the current visit size and 6,000 records per season, the difference is likely small, but it grows with species list length and notes.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+**Description.** Each accepted submission is an immutable complete snapshot of the visit, naming the revision it started from. Revisions form a graph. A revision based on the sole active revision becomes current; a revision based on any other revision becomes a competing branch. A root submission for an existing visit is also a competing branch. The visit is current only while one active revision remains; otherwise it is unresolved and no revision is current. A coordinator resolves by selecting one whole revision; the others remain stored as not selected. There is no field-level merge rule.
+
+**Demonstration.** Revision R1 is current. Worker A submits R2 based on R1, and R2 becomes current. Worker B, offline since before R2, submits R3 based on R1. R3 is a competing branch, so the visit becomes unresolved. A coordinator compares R2 and R3 and selects R2; R3 remains stored as not selected. If B later edits R3 and submits R4 based on R3, that branch reopens and the visit is unresolved again.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- **Scientific safety: strong.** The system never combines fields. Every current value comes from one submitted revision, so a related edit cannot be silently combined with another edit because no combination occurs.
+- **Review workload and export readiness: weak.** Any revision based on a non-active revision creates review work, even when the changed fields are unrelated. More visits remain unresolved and are held out of the Friday export until a coordinator selects a revision.
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden: strong.** No merge rules are needed. The main complexity is the revision graph, active-frontier computation, idempotent submission, and the resolution compare-and-set.
+- **Explainability and provenance: strong.** The exported visit is exactly one revision. The resolution record names the coordinator, the selected revision, and the contributing revisions, so the path from submission to export is inspectable at revision granularity.
+- **Offline capture and retry simplicity: strong.** The tablet needs the base revision ID and a complete draft. A stale base is accepted as a competing branch rather than rejected, so an offline edit is never lost for being old.
+- **Submission size and network cost: partial.** Each edit sends a complete visit snapshot. At current visit size this is small, but it is larger than a change set and grows with the species list and notes.
+
+**Summary.** Preserves whole-visit intent and revision-level audit at the cost of more review work and larger submissions.
+
+### Field-Aware Three-Way Merge
+
+**Description.** Each submission carries the base state or base revision plus structured changes. The server combines changes to disjoint fields automatically and sends overlapping changes to review. It requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries, and it needs field-level provenance to explain a combined record. Tablets retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export gets more visits ready automatically, but its review view must show field-level overlaps.
+
+**Demonstration.** Base revision R1 has observers `[A]` and species entries `{X: 10%, Y: 5%}`. Worker A changes the observer list to `[A, B]`; Worker B changes species X cover to `15%`. A field-aware merge combines both into a current record because the fields are disjoint. If both edit species X cover, the merge flags an overlap for review. If one edits species X identification and another edits X cover, the rule must decide whether those are the same entry or related fields; a coarse rule can combine them without noticing the scientific relationship.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- **Scientific safety: partial.** Disjoint field changes combine, but the system cannot reliably know which fields are scientifically related. Maintained rules reduce the risk but do not eliminate it. A same-entry rule catches some overlaps; cross-field relationships such as species identification and cover, or observer and notes, can remain invisible.
+- **Review workload and export readiness: strong.** Unrelated edits combine automatically, so fewer visits need coordinator attention and more visits are ready for the Friday export.
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden: weak.** The server needs merge rules for every field shape, including ordered observer lists and species entries, plus tests for each rule. Rules must evolve with the field protocol. That is ongoing work for a small team, and the cover encoding and species-entry shape are not yet fixed.
+- **Explainability and provenance: partial.** An exported record may combine several submissions. Explaining each value requires field-level provenance and a merge audit, which is more complex than naming one selected revision.
+- **Offline capture and retry simplicity: partial.** The tablet must retain a base snapshot and compute structured changes. That is more client state and logic than sending a complete snapshot, and stale-base handling is more involved.
+- **Submission size and network cost: strong.** The tablet sends only changes plus a base reference, which is smaller than a complete snapshot. At current visit size, the advantage is modest.
+
+**Summary.** Reduces review work and submission size, but requires maintained merge rules and field-level provenance, and can combine scientifically related edits without noticing.
+
+## Selection
+
+Whole-visit revisions are selected. The policy wins on scientific safety, implementation and maintenance burden, explainability, and offline simplicity. It loses on review workload and submission size. The team accepts the larger review queue over maintaining merge rules that can combine scientifically related edits without noticing.
+
+The decision turns on the tenets *Do not silently discard a submitted edit*, *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*, and *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*. It also preserves *Let field workers continue collecting observations without connectivity*, although both options can satisfy that tenet.
+
+The policy forecloses automatic combination of unrelated edits, a smaller review queue, smaller submissions, and field-level provenance as a first-release requirement. It does not foreclose field-aware merge as a later change.
+
+Field-aware merge remains the credible alternative. Its strongest form merges only fields known to be independent and sends any same-species-entry edit to review, which reduces review volume without claiming to infer scientific relationships. It is the better policy if review volume becomes unmanageable for two coordinators, if full-snapshot submissions become costly as visits grow, or if the team can define and test safe merge rules at species-entry granularity with field-level provenance. Those conditions are the triggers to revisit this decision. The competing option, criteria, and trade-offs are recorded here so a future reader can re-run the judgment rather than reconstruct it from the selected design.
+
+## Downstream Accommodation
+
+### Tablet-Sync
+
+- Retain the base revision ID for every local draft. The tablet does not need a base snapshot or a structured change set for merging; a base snapshot may still be kept for display or local comparison, but it is not required by the submission contract.
+- Submit complete visit snapshots. Local storage holds the complete draft and its base revision ID.
+- Define how a chain of local edits maps to submissions. The tablet can send one latest complete snapshot based on the last known server revision, or submit edits sequentially and use each returned revision ID as the base for the next. Both are valid; the tablet must not assume a stale base is rejected.
+- Handle the acceptance response: revision ID, server receipt time, and visit status. An idempotent replay returns the original acceptance result; fetch current state for the visit's latest status.
+- Treat a submission based on an older revision as accepted and queued for review, not as a failed edit. Show the worker that the visit is unresolved or awaiting coordinator review.
+- Show which revision the local draft is based on. When the visit is unresolved, the current-state response returns the competing active frontier summaries and no current revision.
+- Decide whether to lock editing of an unresolved visit. The record store accepts further revisions and keeps the visit unresolved, so locking is a tablet-sync interface choice, not a server rule.
+- Retry with the same idempotency key. A different payload under the same key is an idempotency conflict and indicates a client bug.
+- Handle rejection cases: invalid payload, unknown plot reference, and unknown base revision. Revisions are retained, so an unknown base is a client or visit-identity error rather than routine expiry.
+- Monitor full-snapshot size and bandwidth. If visits grow enough that full snapshots become costly, that is a trigger to revisit field-aware merge.
+
+### Review-Export
+
+- Build the revision comparison screen from revision history, showing complete competing revisions with author and receipt time. The record store supplies complete payloads; the comparison presentation belongs to review-export.
+- Present the unresolved queue oldest-first. Each visit carries its frontier version and active frontier summaries. Coordinators need triage and filtering as volume grows; pagination and filtering are deferred beyond the first release.
+- Resolve by selecting one revision and sending the reviewed active frontier set plus the frontier version. Handle a stale-review rejection by refreshing the visit and comparing again; a revision that arrived during review must not be silently dropped.
+- Exclude unresolved visits from the CSV. Re-check status at export time, because a late competing revision can reopen a previously current visit before Friday.
+- Export the current revision's complete payload. Resolve plot display metadata through the catalog rather than embedding it in the revision.
+- Show resolution history and audit: coordinator, selected revision, contributing revisions, and resolution time.
+- Do not introduce field-level provenance in the first release. Provenance is revision-level. If the partner or an audit requires field-level provenance, that is a trigger to revisit field-aware merge.
+- For a corrected or combined result, the coordinator selects one revision and submits a follow-up complete revision. If the review screen should author a combined revision directly, that is a separate deferred decision; it would still produce a new complete revision, not an automatic field merge.
+- Do not invent concurrency semantics in the export side. Read status and revisions from the record store and call its resolution operation.
+
+### HLD and Site Catalog
+
+The HLD states the whole-visit revision contract. The site catalog is unaffected: the record store validates plot references against the catalog and retains plot UUIDs in revisions.
+
