+
+A root submission for a visit that does not exist creates the visit and stores the revision as its current revision. A root submission for a visit that already has revisions is accepted as a competing root; the visit becomes unresolved. A base revision may be current, competing, superseded, or not selected. Basing on any known revision of the visit is accepted; basing on a non-active revision creates a competing branch and reopens review rather than rejecting the edit.
+
+### Idempotency
+
+The idempotency key is scoped to the authenticated author. The server stores the accepted key with the revision ID and a canonical hash of the validated submission payload. The canonical form excludes server-assigned fields and is insensitive to serialization order.
+
+- Repeating an accepted key with the same canonical payload returns the original acceptance result and creates no revision.
+- Repeating an accepted key with a different canonical payload is rejected as an idempotency conflict; the original revision is unchanged.
+- A key used by a different author is independent.
+
+The uniqueness constraint on author and key is enforced transactionally, so two concurrent retries of the same submission produce one revision and one replay.
+
+### Retrieval
+
+| Operation | Caller | Result |
+|---|---|---|
+| `GET /visits/{visit_id}` | Authenticated worker or coordinator | Current-state summary. |
+| `GET /visits/{visit_id}/revisions` | Authenticated worker or coordinator | Full revision history, including superseded and not-selected revisions. |
+| `GET /visits/{visit_id}/resolutions` | Authenticated worker or coordinator | Resolution history. |
+| `GET /review/unresolved` | Coordinator | Unresolved visits with their competing active frontier revisions. |
+| `POST /visits/{visit_id}/submissions` | Authenticated worker | Accept a complete revision. |
+| `POST /visits/{visit_id}/resolutions` | Coordinator | Resolve an unresolved visit. |
+
+Current-state retrieval returns `visit_id`, `status`, `frontier_version`, and `has_unresolved_review`. When the visit is `current`, it also returns `current_revision_id` and the current revision's payload, author, and receipt time. When the visit is `unresolved`, it returns the active frontier revision summaries (revision ID, author, receipt time, base revision ID) and does not return a current revision.
+
+Revision-history retrieval returns every revision with its payload, author, receipt time, base revision ID, and revision status. Resolution records are available through the resolution-history operation. The review screen uses this history to compare complete revisions.
+
+The unresolved-review query returns each unresolved visit's `frontier_version` and active frontier revision summaries. It orders visits by the oldest active frontier receipt time so older review work is presented first. Pagination and filtering are not part of the first-release contract.
+
+### Resolution
+
+A coordinator resolves an unresolved visit by selecting one reviewed active frontier revision as the visit's current revision. The record store does not combine fields from different revisions and does not synthesize a new revision during resolution.
+
+A resolution request carries:
+
+| Field | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `winning_revision_id` | The reviewed active frontier revision selected as current. |
+| `reviewed_revision_ids` | The complete active frontier set the coordinator reviewed. |
+| `frontier_version` | The visit's frontier version at the start of review. |
+
+The server verifies that the visit is unresolved, the `frontier_version` matches, `reviewed_revision_ids` equals the current active frontier, and `winning_revision_id` is in that set. If any check fails, the resolution is rejected without changing the visit; a stale review returns the current active frontier and version so the coordinator can compare again.
+
+On success, in one transaction, the winning revision becomes `current`, the other reviewed active frontier revisions become `not_selected`, the visit becomes `current`, and `frontier_version` increments. The server stores a resolution record with the resolution ID, visit ID, coordinator ID, winning revision ID, contributing revision IDs, and server resolution time.
+
+A retry after a successful resolution is rejected as stale or as having no unresolved work; the coordinator refreshes the visit and sees the recorded resolution. A coordinator who needs a corrected or combined result selects one revision, then submits a new complete revision based on it through the normal submission path.
+
+### Authorization and Retention
+
+Authenticated workers may submit and retrieve visits. Only principals with the coordinator role may read the unresolved-review queue or resolve review work. The record store retains every accepted revision, its author, its receipt time, and every resolution for the life of the visit. It does not delete superseded or not-selected revisions.
+
+## Downstream Contract
+
+Tablet-sync retains the base revision ID for each local draft, submits complete visit revisions, and displays the visit state and competing active frontier revisions returned by this node. A submission based on an older revision is accepted and reopens review; tablet-sync must not treat it as a failed edit.
+
+Review-export uses the unresolved-review query to present the coordinator queue, uses revision history to compare complete revisions, and calls the resolution operation. A visit enters the partner export only while its status is `current`; the record store supplies the status and current revision, while review-export owns the CSV and the decision to withhold unresolved visits.
+
+The detailed obligations for both nodes are recorded in the [concurrency decision doc](decisions/whole-visit-revisions.md).
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrency policy ([decision doc](decisions/whole-visit-revisions.md)) | Whole-visit revisions: each accepted submission is an immutable complete revision; competing branches wait for coordinator resolution. | Field-aware three-way merge; last-write-wins; lock the visit during editing. | Whole-visit revisions preserve each worker's submitted visit as a unit and avoid merge rules that can silently combine scientifically related edits. The cost is a larger review queue, which the team accepts. Last-write-wins violates the tenet against silently discarding a submitted edit; locking blocks offline capture. |
+| Conflict detection | Branch position in the revision graph. Any revision based on a non-active revision creates a competing branch, regardless of which fields changed. | Field-level overlap detection; automatic disjoint-field merge. | The record store cannot reliably infer which scientific fields are related. Comparing field names would produce false confidence and hide related edits. |
+| Unresolved visit state | No revision is current while the active frontier has more than one revision. | Keep the latest-arriving revision as current with a warning; keep the pre-conflict revision as current. | Arrival order is not evidence of scientific authority, and an export must not select a winner implicitly. |
+| Resolution outcome | Coordinator selects one active frontier revision as current; other reviewed active frontier revisions become not selected. | Coordinator authors a combined revision during resolution; automatic merge of selected fields. | Selecting a whole revision keeps provenance and intent intact. A coordinator who needs a correction can submit a follow-up complete revision, which remains a normal auditable revision. |
+| Stale submission handling | Accept a revision based on any known revision of the visit and reopen that branch for review. | Reject a submission whose base is no longer an active revision and require the client to rebase. | Rejecting a stale base would discard observations made offline. Reopening review preserves the edit and lets a coordinator compare it. |
+| Resolution race control | Resolution names the reviewed active frontier set and the frontier version; the server applies it only if both still match. | Last-write-wins between coordinators; lock the visit for the duration of review. | A new competing revision can arrive while a coordinator reviews. The compare-and-set prevents that revision from being silently marked not selected. |
+| Initial submission collision | A root submission for an existing visit is accepted as a competing root. | Reject the submission as a duplicate visit; merge it into the existing current revision. | The edit is preserved and reviewed. Rejecting loses the worker's observations; merging would combine visits the server cannot prove are the same. |
+| Idempotency scope | Key scoped to the submitting author, with canonical payload comparison. | Globally scoped key; key scoped to visit. | Author scoping prevents unrelated workers' keys from colliding while keeping retries safe. |
+| Revision identity | Server-issued opaque revision IDs. | Client-generated revision IDs; timestamp-ordered IDs. | Tablets need a stable ID to name a base revision. Opaque server IDs avoid clock authority and client collision. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. ✅ Concurrency policy: whole-visit revisions, with coordinator selection among competing complete revisions. See the [decision doc](decisions/whole-visit-revisions.md).
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Whether the review screen should support authoring a combined revision directly, rather than selecting one revision and submitting a follow-up. The current contract supports the follow-up path.
+2. Whether a revision may change the visit's plot reference. The current contract treats the plot reference as an editable field of the complete visit snapshot.
+3. Retention and compaction of revision history beyond the first release.
+4. Pagination, filtering, and prioritization of the unresolved-review queue beyond oldest-first ordering.
+5. Whether tablet-sync should lock editing of an unresolved visit in its interface. The record store accepts further revisions and keeps the visit unresolved.
+6. The concrete encoding of species cover values. The record store validates entry shape but leaves the field protocol to the capture and export designs.
